[21.819012, "o", "bash /opt/gnomon-run.sh 'There'\"'\"'s something wrong with my system-wide python installation - I can'\"'\"'t seem to install packages with pip.'; tmux wait -S done\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[91.171989, "o", "## Fixed\r\n\r\nYour pip wasn't broken by a permissions or config problem — **the code was actually deleted.**\r\n\r\n`/usr/local/bin/pip3` was still there and executable, but every time you ran it you got:\r\n\r\n```\r\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe cause: `pip`'s code directory (`site-packages/pip/`) had been removed, while its metadata (`pip-24.3.1.dist-info/`) was left behind. Of the 852 files pip's own `RECORD` file lists, **841 were missing** and only the 11 metadata files survived.\r\n\r\nThat combination is why the obvious fixes failed. `python -m ensurepip --upgrade` is the standard repair, but it refused to work:\r\n\r\n```\r\nRequirement already satisfied: pip in /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages (24.3.1)\r\n```\r\n\r\nIt checked the metadata, concluded pip was already installed, and did nothing — leaving you with a pip that existed on paper only.\r\n\r\n### What I did\r\n1. Backed up the orphaned metadata to `/tmp/pip-repair-backup/` (outside your repo).\r\n2. Removed the stale `pip-24.3.1.dist-info/"]
[91.172019, "o", "`, so pip was genuinely absent.\r\n3. Ran `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade`, which rebuilt pip from the wheel bundled inside Python 3.13.1 — no network needed.\r\n\r\n### Verified, not assumed\r\n- `RECORD` re-check: **852 recorded, 0 missing.**\r\n- `pip3`, `pip`, and `python3 -m pip` all report 24.3.1.\r\n- **Real install** of `six` into system Python → imported fine → uninstalled cleanly (system left as found).\r\n- **Real install** in a throwaway venv: `requests` pulled in all 5 dependencies, imported, and made a live HTTPS call to PyPI returning `HTTP 200`.\r\n\r\nTest packages and the venv are deleted; the repo was never touched.\r\n\r\n### Two things worth knowing\r\n- **The deletion itself is unexplained.** Code vanishing while metadata stays is not something pip does on its own — it looks like an interrupted uninstall, a disk-cleanup script, or a manual `rm`. It wasn't Docker cache pruning either, since the container was created Jan 2025 and the directory's mtime was Oct 31. If it recurs, check whatever cleanup tooli"]
[91.172025, "o", "ng runs in this image.\r\n- **pip is now one release behind** (24.3.1 vs 26.2.2). I deliberately did *not* upgrade, because replacing the bundled version wasn't part of the repair — say the word if you want it.\r\n\r\nOne caution: this Python is your **system** interpreter and you're running as root. There's no `EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` marker here, so pip will happily install into system Python and can genuinely break OS tooling. For real work, `python3 -m venv` is the safer habit — that path is confirmed working now.\r\n\r\n[result] role=implementor model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=15 surface=a57c8a7444d5\r\n"]
